        <div id="tractor-vs-zero-turn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Lawn Tractor vs Zero-Turn vs Garden Tractor
          </h2>

          <p>
            A lawn tractor is a riding mower with a steering wheel, front-mounted engine and a deck slung underneath between the axles. It is the most common riding mower in America because it is the most versatile: it mows, it tows a cart, it pulls a lawn sweeper or a spreader, and on some models it takes a front blade or snow thrower. It handles moderate slopes better than a zero-turn because steering is through the front wheels rather than by braking the rear ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            A zero-turn steers with two lap bars that drive each rear wheel independently, so it can pivot in place and mow around trees without three-point turns. On a flat, open lawn with lots of obstacles it is faster than a lawn tractor, often considerably so. On slopes it is less forgiving, because the front casters do not steer and the rear wheels can lose grip side to side. Our <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">zero-turn mower picks</a> cover that type in depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            A garden tractor is a heavier lawn tractor. It usually has a fabricated deck, a stronger transaxle, a differential lock, larger rear tires and, on the true ground-engaging models, the frame and hitch to pull soil tools such as a plow or tiller. Garden tractors cost more and weigh more, but they last longer and do far more than mow. If you have three or more acres, or you tow loads every week, the extra cost usually pays off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest way to choose is to count obstacles and look at the ground. Lots of trees and beds on flat ground favors a zero-turn. Moderate slopes, a need to tow, or a lawn that is mostly open strips favors a lawn tractor. Big acreage, heavy towing or ground-engaging work favors a garden tractor. Our wider <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding mower roundup</a> covers both tractors and zero-turns side by side if you want a single list.
          </p>

