        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Deck Size and Acreage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck width does more for mowing time than horsepower does. A 42-inch deck covers each pass about 10 percent faster than a 38-inch deck, and a 54-inch deck covers each pass nearly 30 percent faster than a 42-inch one. On a two-acre lawn that difference turns a two-hour job into a ninety-minute one, every week of the season.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a starting point, manufacturers and dealers commonly pair a 42-inch deck with up to about one and a half acres, a 46- to 48-inch deck with roughly one to two acres, and a 50- to 54-inch deck with two acres and up. Those are not hard limits. A lawn with long open runs mows faster than its acreage suggests, and a lawn broken up by beds, trees and fences mows slower, so adjust up or down for your layout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before you buy the widest deck you can afford, measure two things. The first is your narrowest gate or path, because a 54-inch deck plus its discharge chute will not fit through a 4-foot gate. The second is your storage space, because a garden tractor with a 54-inch deck needs roughly a full single-car-garage bay once you allow room to walk around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turning radius matters too. Cub Cadet lists a 16-inch radius on the XT1 LT46 and Craftsman lists 18 inches on the T110, while larger garden tractors turn wider. On a lawn full of trees and beds a tight radius saves more time than an extra few inches of deck. If your yard is genuinely large and open, our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-large-yard/">guide to mowers for large yards</a> compares tractors against zero-turns and wide walk-behinds by acreage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="engines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Engines: Single vs Twin, and What Horsepower Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Horsepower is the least useful number on a lawn tractor spec sheet. Engine makers rate gross horsepower at a specific speed in lab conditions, so two engines with the same rating can behave very differently cutting thick wet grass. What predicts longevity and smoothness is the engine design, not the headline number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Single-cylinder engines, like the Briggs and Stratton unit Craftsman lists for the T110, are cheaper to build and lighter, and they are adequate on a flat, regularly mowed lawn. They vibrate more and many use splash lubrication rather than a pressurized oil system, which tends to shorten life under heavy use.
          </p>

          <p>
            V-twin engines, like the Kawasaki and Kohler twins on our other gas picks, run smoother, hold speed better under load and usually have full-pressure lubrication with a spin-on oil filter. John Deere's listings for the X350 and X380 both call out full-pressure lube and an oil filter, and the X380's listing adds cast-iron cylinder liners, which resist wear. That combination is the strongest single predictor of a tractor that is still running in year twelve.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand matters less than it once did, but parts availability matters a lot. Kawasaki, Kohler and Briggs and Stratton engines all have deep parts networks in the US. Before buying, check that a dealer or small-engine shop near you services the engine you are buying, because a tractor that waits three weeks for a part in June is a tractor you resent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transmission" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hydrostatic vs Gear Drive
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every lawn tractor above the entry level now uses a hydrostatic transmission. It works like a hydraulic pump and motor rather than a set of gears, so you change speed smoothly with a pedal or lever without stopping or using a clutch. On a lawn with lots of trees and beds that is a real time saver, and it is easier for newer drivers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gear-drive tractors, like the Craftsman T110 with its 7-speed manual transmission, make you stop to change gears. They are cheaper, simpler and fine for open lawns where you pick one speed and stay in it, but they are tiring on a busy lawn. They also tow less smoothly, because you cannot feather speed under load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not all hydrostatic transaxles are equal. Light-duty units are sized for mowing and occasional light towing, while heavier units such as the K58 that John Deere lists on the X380 are built for more load and more hours. Garden tractors go further still, often adding a differential lock like the foot-pedal rear diff lock Cub Cadet lists on the XT2 GX54, which keeps both rear wheels pulling on wet grass or when towing uphill.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you tow regularly, pull a heavy cart of firewood or mulch, or mow on slopes, prioritize the transaxle over the engine. Transaxle failure is the repair that most often makes an older tractor not worth fixing, so a heavier unit is worth paying for. Our <a href="/riding-lawn-mower-maintenance/">riding mower maintenance schedule</a> covers the checks that help any transaxle last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Stamped vs Fabricated Decks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most lawn tractors use a stamped deck, pressed from a single sheet of steel. Stamped decks are lighter, cheaper and perfectly adequate for mowing a residential lawn. Their weakness is rust: grass clippings hold moisture against the steel, and over years the deck thins and cracks, especially along the welds and around the spindles.
          </p>

          <p>
            A fabricated deck is welded from thicker plate steel, often 10 or 11 gauge. Cub Cadet lists the XT2 GX54 D FAB's deck as 11-gauge fabricated steel with a limited lifetime warranty on the deck shell, which tells you how confident the maker is in it. Fabricated decks resist impact from roots and stumps far better and usually outlast the tractor they are mounted on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck depth matters as well. Deeper decks, like the Accel Deep design John Deere uses on the X350 and X380, give clippings more room to circulate before they are discharged, which helps in thick or wet grass and improves bagging. Shallow decks clog sooner and leave more windrows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever deck you buy, clean the underside after mowing, keep the blades sharp, and check the deck level each spring. A dull or unbalanced blade tears grass and stresses the spindles. Our guides to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-blades/">best lawn mower blades</a> and <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/">how often to sharpen them</a> cover both jobs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attachments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Towing, Bagging and Attachments
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reason many people buy a lawn tractor rather than a zero-turn is the hitch. A rear hitch turns the tractor into a yard workhorse: a dump cart for mulch and firewood, a lawn sweeper for leaves, a broadcast spreader for fertilizer, a dethatcher or aerator for spring renovation. John Deere lists 600 pounds of towing capacity for the X350 and Ryobi lists 500 pounds for its 42-inch battery tractor, which is plenty for a loaded cart on level ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bagging is the other common add-on. Most tractors take a two- or three-bin rear bagger, sold separately. Bagging is slow, because you stop to empty every few passes, so most owners mulch or side-discharge most of the season and bag only in leaf season or for a tidy finish. Deep decks and fabricated decks generally bag better because the airflow is stronger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Front attachments such as a dozer blade or a snow thrower are where lawn tractors and garden tractors split. Many lawn tractors can take a front blade, but snow throwers and ground-engaging tools usually need a garden tractor's heavier frame and transaxle. If winter snow clearing is part of your plan, check the attachment list for the exact model before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight and traction limit everything. A lawn tractor towing a heavy cart down a slope can be pushed by the load, so stay well within rated towing capacity, use wheel weights or chains if the maker offers them, and never tow on slopes steeper than the manual allows. Our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">guide to mowers for hills</a> goes into slope safety in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Warranty, Maintenance and Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read the warranty before the spec sheet. A warranty with an hour limit, like John Deere's 4-year or 300-hour bumper-to-bumper coverage on the X350 and X380, ends at whichever limit you reach first, so a heavy user may run out of hours before years. A warranty with no hour limit, like the 3-year coverage Cub Cadet lists on the XT1 LT46 and XT2 GX54, is more forgiving for big lawns. Also check whether the powertrain is included and whether a dealer must do the repairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Routine maintenance is the same on every gas tractor: change the oil and filter on the schedule in the manual, replace the air filter and spark plug, sharpen the blades, check belt wear and tire pressure, and keep the battery charged over winter. Most owners can do all of it with basic tools. A battery tractor like the Ryobi skips the engine items but still needs blade, belt and tire care.
          </p>

          <p>
            How long a lawn tractor lasts depends mostly on the class. Entry-level single-cylinder tractors often reach five to eight seasons, mid-range twin-cylinder hydrostatic tractors commonly reach ten to twelve, and garden tractors with fabricated decks and heavy transaxles can run fifteen or more with care. Storage under cover and regular deck cleaning add years to all of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            When something major fails out of warranty, compare the repair cost with the tractor's value. A new engine or transaxle on a budget tractor often costs more than half of a replacement, while the same repair on a premium tractor is usually worth doing. Our <a href="/riding-lawn-mower-maintenance/">maintenance schedule</a> and our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-riding-lawn-mowers-last/">how long riding mowers last</a> cover both in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Horsepower Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gross horsepower is measured in lab conditions and says little about how a tractor mows thick grass or how long it lasts. A twin-cylinder engine with full-pressure lubrication and an oil filter on a strong hydrostatic transaxle matters far more than two extra rated horsepower.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Gate Width</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 54-inch deck will not fit through a 4-foot gate, and you only find out after delivery. Measure your narrowest gate, path and shed door before choosing a deck size, and allow a few inches for the discharge chute.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Gear Drive for a Busy Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A manual gear transmission is cheaper, but on a lawn full of trees and beds you will stop to change speed dozens of times a week. Unless your lawn is open and simple, the extra cost of hydrostatic drive pays for itself in time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overloading the Hitch on Slopes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rated towing capacity assumes level ground. A loaded cart can push a tractor downhill and cause a loss of control. Stay well under the rating on slopes, use the maker's wheel weights if offered, and follow the slope limits in the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Warranty Fine Print</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some warranties end at an hour limit before the years run out, and some exclude the powertrain or require dealer service. Read whether the coverage has an hour cap and what it covers before comparing prices.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

