        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best lawn tractor for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For one to two acres, the John Deere X350 is our top pick for its Kawasaki V-twin, K46 hydrostatic transaxle and 4-year or 300-hour warranty, per dealer listings. The Husqvarna TS 146X is the better value if you want a 46-inch deck for less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a lawn tractor and a garden tractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A garden tractor is a heavier lawn tractor, usually with a fabricated deck, stronger transaxle, differential lock and bigger rear tires. It tows heavier loads and some models pull ground-engaging tools. Lawn tractors are lighter and cheaper, and built mainly for mowing and light towing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a lawn tractor or a zero-turn better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A zero-turn is faster on flat lawns with many obstacles. A lawn tractor is better on moderate slopes, easier for new drivers and far more useful for towing carts, spreaders and sweepers. Our zero-turn vs riding mower guide compares them in detail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size lawn tractor do I need for 2 acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 46- to 48-inch deck is a sensible minimum for two acres, and a 50- to 54-inch deck mows it noticeably faster. The John Deere X380 with a 48-inch deck or a 54-inch garden tractor are good fits, depending on your gates and storage space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric lawn tractors any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Battery lawn tractors such as the Ryobi 80V 42-inch are quiet and need no oil changes, and Ryobi rates that model for up to 2 acres per charge. Runtime drops in tall or wet grass, and the price is higher than a comparable gas tractor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a lawn tractor last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Entry-level tractors often last five to eight seasons, mid-range twin-cylinder hydrostatic tractors ten to twelve, and garden tractors with fabricated decks fifteen or more with good care. Covered storage and regular maintenance make the biggest difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is hydrostatic transmission worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most lawns, yes. Hydrostatic drive lets you change speed smoothly with a pedal or lever without stopping, which saves time around trees and beds and makes towing smoother. Gear drive only makes sense on open, simple lawns where you mow at one speed.
